{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "04:33", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "Great, thanks. Well, thank you for that. I really appreciate you guys also taking the time to meet with me on short notice. You guys probably have a very busy schedule with all the stuff that you're trying to do. So a quick background. Venkat Gulapalli. I am a pediatrician by training, only practiced for a couple years, but had been in the healthcare marketing space for the last 20 plus years. Of the last 15 or 16 or so, have been really around data, customer data, and when I say customer, really around the HCP, some patient, but really around the customer data and creating unique data products and platforms and ecosystems that essentially support brands. Specifically in the last four or five years it's been centered around rare disease. And then also I have a bit of a, just a zigzag.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["client"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "05:42", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "I worked with Disney for about almost two years. I led the design and development of an identity graph for them, which is consolidating all of their customer data across Disney, Hulu, Disney, Hulu, ESPN plus and all of their digital properties into one master global database so that we can create these single views, advanced analytics and capabilities and ultimately improve subscriber growth. Over the last two, three years, my focus has really been around developing data strategy frameworks that allow brands and any kind of stakeholder that works with the brands to leverage these granular analytics profiling techniques. And these frameworks are designed to be repeatable, scalable and very adaptable in terms of the growing needs of any organization. So I worked with anything from small to midsize agencies to large enterprise companies designing and implementing these frameworks.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["teaching"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "09:40", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "So, this was the culmination end deliverable, along with another piece that contained all of the providers and the institutions that we've identified during this entire process. So the goal was really here to help Greg at that time, take their product, identify ideal clinical trial sites. Without understanding, how much data we have, how do we analyze the data, what frameworks, without having anything to start with. So, what we first did was really try to understand what the end goal was. Identify clinical trial sites, investigators, and through that process, the number of providers that are managing these patients with this rare disease called RDEB, and then also 20 ideal clinical sites for the trials, for the phase iii trials, and then backup sites and centers and providers. So go ahead.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["teaching"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "11:56", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "We also ruled out differential diagnosis and then weighted each of these factors on its own and gave it a certain weight, so that as the algorithm processed this, we could get a lot more specific in terms of how confident we are of the results. And a lot of this is not something that you could typically put together in a week or two. This is a lot of this leveraged my background as a physician and how they think and how they juggle all of these factors in assessing and diagnosing and treating. Long story short, what were able to do is create an algorithm that analyzed all of this, ran the weighted data points, and identified the exact number of physicians that generated this data.", "bucket": "line", "tags": ["blunt"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "12:49", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "And what that means is those that billed for that specific diagnostic gene test, or that did that specific skin biopsy, or that identified a set of clinical features, not all of them, but they had to have one or two minimum to qualify, and then also any sort of treatment that they were just ad hoc just for the pain and the scarring and the discomfort. So what were able to find out of this is the exact number of providers that were managing patients with this condition, as well as the number of patients that existed in the US. But as a part of that process, we encountered other things that we need to tackle, which the end goal was identify ideal clinical trial sites for phase three.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["teaching"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "18:02", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "It goes on, but were able to, in detail, provide a set number of providers that would make ideal clinical investigators for the ideal sites. And we created about, I think, ten backups for each in relation to what you're looking for. Is all of this was generated within, I think, I want to say, four to five week period, Greg, or.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["blunt"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "25:12", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "Yeah, and so I wouldn't say this is a very AI. This example is very AI driven. We used advanced clustering algorithms and then we used some scoring models. And I am not the data science guy. I work with a group of data scientists to implement this. Let me see if I can find.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["blunt"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "28:10", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "And you'll see that the patient count came down as well. And then we applied the diagnostic criteria before at least one of the diagnostic tests because any one of those was almost like a gold standard and diagnostic of that condition. And we found 579 providers that managed 4000 some odd patients. And we just went on to profile all of these folks. We even validated these findings. And we, for example, if we identify the overall RDEV experts like James Feinstein, I think either you or I think the medical director, Greg, randomly reached out to some of these folks and confirmed these counts at some point. I think they were off by like one or two here and there.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["blunt"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "30:45", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "Right. And we even identified some providers that we didn't. That was a surprise for us and we realized some specialties that were seeing these patients that we didn't account for. But it was an interesting exercise. But to your point, Doug. Yes, we did this in a very short turnaround. Is that an ideal environment to do something like this? No, but just given the things that were working with at that time and the deliverable in the sense of urgency, we felt that the results were almost close to real world.", "bucket": "line", "tags": ["blunt"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "32:24", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "Yeah. And I like that approach and I always use that, which is the lowest common denominator. At minimum, we're validating some of our initial assumptions and hypotheses and in a best case scenario, we're gaining new insights and a new approach.", "bucket": "definition", "tags": ["teaching"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "35:12", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "And I have a more recent one as well, where we did an assessment with another rare disease product. All right, so this was for Spark. This again was probably about the same timeframe. The client was an existing. We were an agency of record. The agency was an agency of record. I think it was May or June. They came to us and said, hey, I need in four weeks a complete assessment of everything I've done for the last two, three years and tell me what's working and what's not for both patients and providers as well as sales and marketing. So it was roughly 250 assets, five or six channels, and we had to go through six, seven different vendors, partners. Yeah. So we utilized a similar algorithm.", "bucket": "voice", "tags": ["teaching"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "37:20", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "But what we found is, was that there were certain assets by certain vendors and partners that did so well. We had to create new stages between the awareness and the retention because, and we call them micro conversions. And macro conversions and supplemental conversions.", "bucket": "definition", "tags": ["teaching"]}
{"transcript_id": "4974-Hollice-Ct-2-m4a - 2024-12-13", "timestamp": "40:43", "speaker": "VENKAT", "quote": "And the great thing about what I just shared this framework from a big picture, is that since we've done this and we've done many after this, but these are the best examples, they are now repeatable and scalable. So if you ever wanted to deploy this for a new client using the same framework, you can hit the ground running in days and not weeks or months.", "bucket": "line", "tags": ["client"]}

